# Integration and market-consolidation strategy for a pan-African banking acquisition in 2026

## Executive recommendation

Adopt a **federated banking model with a common digital layer**, rather than forcing the Nigerian, Kenyan, and Moroccan banks onto one core-banking system or one operating culture immediately.

The group should:

1. Keep each bank as a locally licensed, locally governed entity accountable to its domestic supervisor.
2. Stabilize operations, cyber controls, liquidity, regulatory reporting, and customer service before pursuing major migrations.
3. Establish a group integration office, but give each country CEO and board explicit responsibility for local regulatory commitments and customer continuity.
4. Standardize first at the layers customers and the group can share safely:
   - digital experience and design standards;
   - API management;
   - identity and access controls;
   - cybersecurity;
   - product components;
   - financial-crime controls;
   - management information definitions;
   - selected procurement and technology platforms.
5. Retain local cores initially behind a standardized integration layer. Migrate or replace a core only when the business case, operational readiness, data quality, and regulator engagement support it.
6. Launch a common product “chassis,” not identical products. Pricing, disclosures, payments, credit policies, languages, data locations, and customer-support arrangements must remain country-specific.
7. Make brand migration conditional on customer trust, acquired-brand strength, legal approvals, and service stability. Begin with an endorsed brand and move to a single group brand only where research supports it.
8. Align management through decision rights, incentives, talent reviews, and joint delivery teams—not through immediate replacement of local leadership.
9. Sequence integration through regulatory and customer-risk gates, with independent assurance before any critical data or account migration.
10. Measure success through service continuity, customer retention, regulatory confidence, and risk-adjusted synergy realization—not merely the speed of core conversion or rebranding.

The operating principle should be:

> **Unify what creates scale and control; localize what carries a banking license, regulatory obligation, customer promise, or market advantage.**

---

# 1. Define the integration thesis before selecting systems

The group should first determine which capabilities genuinely need to be common.

A pan-African bank does not need one physical core to offer a recognizable digital experience across countries. Conversely, a common mobile-app interface does not make the underlying products or regulatory obligations identical.

## Capabilities to standardize at group level

Subject to local law and supervisory approval:

- cyber-security standards and security operations;
- identity and privileged-access management;
- API and integration standards;
- software-development and release controls;
- vendor-risk management;
- cloud and outsourcing control frameworks;
- data definitions and management reporting;
- financial-crime minimum standards;
- sanctions and watchlist infrastructure;
- fraud intelligence;
- customer-experience and accessibility standards;
- product-component design;
- treasury and capital reporting methodologies;
- procurement categories with genuine scale benefits;
- disaster-recovery and operational-resilience methodologies.

## Capabilities that should remain locally accountable

- legal-entity balance sheets and books of record;
- regulatory reporting;
- capital, liquidity, recovery, and resolution arrangements;
- customer funds and deposit-protection treatment;
- local payment-scheme participation;
- credit approvals under local delegations;
- local product terms, prices, and disclosures;
- complaints and conduct management;
- data residency or cross-border transfer compliance;
- regulator communications;
- labor and employee-relations decisions;
- tax and statutory reporting;
- language and customer-service requirements;
- market-specific partnerships.

## Capabilities that can be shared but require local configuration

- mobile and internet banking;
- customer onboarding;
- customer relationship management;
- transaction monitoring;
- credit decisioning;
- digital lending;
- payments orchestration;
- collections;
- call-center tools;
- analytics;
- loyalty programs;
- merchant products;
- customer notifications.

This distinction should prevent the integration program from treating “standardization” as an instruction to erase local obligations.

---

# 2. Establish a federated target operating model

## Legal and governance structure

The group should maintain each bank as a separate regulated subsidiary unless a local legal-entity restructuring has a clear rationale and receives all necessary approvals.

Each local board should retain genuine oversight of:

- capital and liquidity;
- risk appetite;
- regulatory compliance;
- internal controls;
- outsourcing;
- material technology changes;
- customer conduct;
- recovery and resolution planning;
- related-party and intragroup arrangements.

Group policies should establish minimum standards, but local boards should be able to require stricter controls where local law or market conditions demand them.

## Decision-rights model

Use four categories:

| Decision category | Primary authority |
|---|---|
| Local license, capital, liquidity, regulatory reporting, customer conduct | Local board and country management |
| Group risk limits, cyber minimums, brand standards, architecture principles | Group board and group functions, with local adoption |
| Shared platforms and major migrations | Joint approval through group and local governance |
| Local pricing, channels, partnerships, language, and product configuration | Country management within group guardrails |

A formal conflict-resolution process should apply where group efficiency objectives conflict with local regulatory or fiduciary obligations. The local entity’s legal and regulatory duties must prevail.

## Integration governance

Create a **Group Integration and Transformation Office**, reporting to a board integration committee and the group CEO.

It should include:

- country integration directors;
- technology and data leads;
- operations;
- risk and compliance;
- finance and treasury;
- legal and regulatory affairs;
- human resources;
- customer experience;
- communications and brand;
- internal audit or independent assurance observers.

The office should maintain:

- one dependency map;
- one regulatory-commitment register;
- one customer-risk register;
- one synergy ledger;
- country-specific implementation plans;
- explicit migration gates;
- issue-escalation protocols.

Do not let the technology program office become the de facto owner of regulatory, product, or customer decisions.

---

# 3. Use a phased integration sequence

## Phase 0: Pre-close and legal Day 1 preparation

Where closing has not yet occurred, complete:

- change-of-control approvals and conditions;
- competition and consumer-protection analysis;
- fit-and-proper reviews for directors and senior managers;
- deposit-protection notifications;
- capital, liquidity, and intragroup funding analysis;
- data-transfer and outsourcing assessments;
- critical vendor and software-license reviews;
- cyber compromise assessments;
- financial-crime and sanctions exposure reviews;
- related-party agreement design;
- employee consultation and labor-law requirements;
- customer and market communication plans.

No system consolidation should be assumed in the acquisition model until local outsourcing, data, reporting, and supervisory constraints are understood.

## Phase 1: Day 1 through approximately 100 days—control and continuity

The first objective is to avoid harm.

### Immediate priorities

- preserve customer account numbers and access channels;
- retain payment-scheme and correspondent-bank connectivity;
- secure privileged accounts and administrator credentials;
- confirm liquidity and intraday-payment controls;
- reconcile customer, general-ledger, and regulatory balances;
- validate backup restoration and disaster recovery;
- assess core and channel capacity;
- identify unsupported software and critical vendor dependencies;
- review fraud rules and suspicious-activity backlogs;
- confirm deposit-insurance communications;
- preserve local regulatory-reporting teams;
- establish regulator-specific communication routines;
- freeze unnecessary high-risk system changes.

### Day 100 outputs

Each country should have:

1. a verified system and data inventory;
2. a critical-process map;
3. a cyber and operational-resilience remediation plan;
4. a product and fee inventory;
5. a customer-segment and profitability baseline;
6. a management and talent assessment;
7. a brand-equity study;
8. a regulatory obligations matrix;
9. a core-banking disposition recommendation;
10. a three-year country integration plan.

During this period, the group should avoid changing the legal bank name, core system, customer credentials, branch network, and principal digital channel simultaneously.

## Phase 2: Months 3–12—connect, control, and simplify

Build the common control and integration foundation while local systems remain operational.

Priorities should include:

- group identity and access management;
- endpoint, network, and security monitoring integration;
- common cyber-incident escalation;
- API gateways around local cores;
- standardized customer and product identifiers;
- group management-information definitions;
- regulatory data lineage;
- fraud and financial-crime control uplift;
- common software-development controls;
- vendor rationalization where low risk;
- product-catalog simplification;
- centralized procurement for non-customer-critical services;
- employee mobility and joint leadership programs.

Customer-facing changes should be limited to improvements that can be delivered without destabilizing the ledger.

## Phase 3: Months 9–24—launch the common digital product chassis

Introduce a group digital platform capable of supporting country-specific configurations.

The platform should separate:

### Common components

- interface design;
- authentication framework;
- device management;
- notification services;
- API orchestration;
- service monitoring;
- analytics instrumentation;
- secure software-development pipeline;
- common support tooling;
- reusable payment, savings, lending, and merchant components.

### Country configurations

- language;
- currency;
- customer identity requirements;
- consent wording;
- product terms;
- fees and taxes;
- interest calculations;
- payment rails;
- transaction limits;
- credit bureau interfaces;
- complaint routes;
- data-storage and retention rules;
- regulatory reports;
- deposit-insurance disclosures.

The group could use a common application codebase with country-specific releases or modules, but should not require all countries to release simultaneously. A defective launch in one market should not force a delay—or create an outage—in the others.

## Phase 4: Months 18–36 or later—selective core migration

Only after the common integration layer is functioning should the group determine whether to:

1. retain and modernize the local core;
2. upgrade it;
3. move it to a managed deployment;
4. migrate to another existing group core;
5. implement a new regional core platform.

The answer may differ by country.

A migration should proceed only if:

- local supervisory engagement is satisfactory;
- the target platform meets local regulatory and payment requirements;
- customer and product data are sufficiently clean;
- product simplification is substantially complete;
- regulatory reports have been run in parallel;
- reconciliation has passed defined thresholds;
- disaster recovery has been tested;
- operational staff are trained;
- customer communications are ready;
- rollback or contingency arrangements are credible;
- an independent readiness assessment approves the cutover.

A single “all countries by one date” core-migration deadline should be avoided.

## Phase 5: Months 24–48—deeper consolidation

After service and control stability:

- rationalize overlapping product variants;
- consolidate selected processing operations;
- optimize branches and service points;
- deepen shared-service arrangements;
- integrate cross-border corporate and trade services;
- establish group customer propositions for eligible clients;
- reassess whether full brand unification creates value;
- retire legacy systems and duplicate data stores.

---

# 4. Technology strategy: common architecture, not immediate common core

## Target architecture

Use five layers:

### 1. Local systems of record

Initially retain each bank’s core ledger, deposits, lending, local payments, and statutory books.

### 2. Country integration layer

Provide adapters between the local core and group services. This layer should handle:

- product and account APIs;
- payment initiation and status;
- balances and transaction history;
- customer servicing;
- event streaming;
- country-specific transformations;
- security and consent enforcement.

### 3. Group digital-services layer

Host reusable components such as:

- authentication;
- customer communications;
- digital document services;
- fraud signals;
- workflow;
- analytics;
- product presentation;
- common user-experience components.

### 4. Local regulatory and data layer

Maintain country-specific reporting, retention, privacy controls, model governance, and supervisory access.

### 5. Group reporting layer

Receive authorized, governed information for consolidated risk, capital, finance, audit, and performance reporting.

This architecture creates unified digital products without making every customer transaction dependent on one cross-border core.

## Data strategy

The group should classify data into:

- locally restricted personal or bank-secrecy data;
- data permitted for cross-border transfer with safeguards;
- pseudonymized group risk and analytics data;
- aggregated management data;
- public or non-sensitive reference data.

For every data flow, document:

- source and destination;
- purpose;
- legal basis;
- consent where required;
- storage location;
- retention period;
- access rights;
- encryption;
- onward transfers;
- regulator or customer restrictions;
- deletion and correction process.

A regional data lake should not be populated simply because technology permits it. Where cross-border use is constrained, deploy local analytics and transfer only permitted outputs or aggregated information.

## Core-migration decision scorecard

Score each bank against:

- system stability;
- regulatory fit;
- functional gaps;
- vendor viability;
- cybersecurity exposure;
- cost to maintain;
- integration capability;
- data quality;
- local skills;
- product complexity;
- migration cost and risk;
- expected synergy;
- time before major end-of-support deadlines.

If an existing core is stable, compliant, API-capable, and cost-effective, retention may be superior to replacement.

## Migration method

For any core conversion:

1. simplify products and close dormant technical variants;
2. cleanse and map customer data;
3. create immutable source-to-target rules;
4. run multiple trial conversions;
5. reconcile accounts, interest, fees, limits, collateral, and general ledger;
6. run regulatory reporting in parallel;
7. test historic statements and complaint evidence;
8. rehearse rollback and business-continuity procedures;
9. use staged migration where platform design allows it;
10. provide enhanced customer support before and after cutover.

No customer should lose transaction history, mandate information, beneficiary records, collateral status, complaint evidence, or legally required documentation because these are difficult to migrate.

---

# 5. Country-specific regulatory and market approach

Exact requirements should be confirmed with local counsel and through direct supervisory engagement. The primary banking supervisor is only one of several relevant authorities.

## Nigeria

### Regulatory engagement

Key authorities and frameworks are likely to include:

- Central Bank of Nigeria;
- Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation;
- Nigeria Data Protection Commission;
- competition and consumer-protection authorities;
- securities, payments, telecommunications, or other authorities where the business model requires them.

Engage early on:

- change of control and governance;
- capital and liquidity;
- cyber resilience;
- outsourcing and cloud use;
- data transfers;
- consumer protection;
- payment-system connectivity;
- financial-crime controls;
- service continuity during migration.

### Market priorities

Nigeria’s scale and high digital transaction volumes make availability, fraud prevention, and payment resilience especially important.

Prioritize:

- reliable mobile and USSD or other appropriate low-bandwidth access;
- instant-payment resilience;
- fraud and account-takeover controls;
- merchant and SME services;
- fast but well-controlled onboarding;
- transparent fees;
- strong service recovery;
- English as the formal base, supplemented by relevant local-language support where customer research justifies it.

Do not migrate the Nigerian core merely because it is the largest synergy opportunity. Its scale means a failed conversion could create the group’s largest operational and reputational loss.

### Likely sequencing

Nigeria may justify early deployment of common cyber, fraud, API, and digital components, while postponing ledger migration until capacity, reconciliation, and peak-volume performance have been proven.

## Kenya

### Regulatory engagement

Relevant authorities are likely to include:

- Central Bank of Kenya;
- Kenya Deposit Insurance Corporation;
- Office of the Data Protection Commissioner;
- Competition Authority of Kenya;
- capital-markets or communications authorities where applicable.

Key topics should include:

- material outsourcing;
- digital credit and customer treatment;
- credit reporting;
- mobile-money and payment integrations;
- data protection;
- operational resilience;
- agent or third-party channels;
- complaints and collections practices.

### Market priorities

Kenyan customers generally expect strong interoperability between bank accounts, mobile-money ecosystems, merchant payments, and digital credit services.

Prioritize:

- mobile-first account servicing;
- mobile-money and instant-payment integration;
- transparent digital-lending terms;
- responsible affordability and collections controls;
- SME cash-flow products;
- English and Kiswahili support;
- agent and merchant-channel reliability.

The group should not replace successful local payment journeys with a generic group design that adds steps or reduces interoperability.

### Likely sequencing

Kenya may be a suitable early market for the common digital chassis if APIs are mature and the acquired bank’s mobile ecosystem is strong. The local payment and mobile-money orchestration layer should remain country-specific even if the user interface and reusable product components are shared.

## Morocco

### Regulatory engagement

Relevant authorities are likely to include:

- Bank Al-Maghrib;
- the national data-protection authority, CNDP;
- the Competition Council;
- capital-markets and other specialist authorities where applicable;
- applicable deposit-protection and banking-sector arrangements.

Engagement should address:

- governance and control by a foreign banking group;
- intragroup outsourcing;
- cross-border data flows;
- operational resilience;
- customer and banking confidentiality;
- accounting and prudential reporting;
- financial-crime controls;
- language and disclosure requirements;
- planned brand or legal-name changes.

### Market priorities

Morocco requires a distinct language and service design rather than translation of an Anglophone product.

Prioritize:

- Arabic and French service journeys, with appropriate consideration of Darija and Amazigh accessibility based on channel and customer segment;
- branch and relationship-manager continuity;
- secure digital onboarding;
- retail, SME, remittance, and trade-finance propositions;
- clear local disclosures;
- careful handling of cross-border data and outsourced processing.

### Likely sequencing

Morocco should receive a localized digital front end and group control standards, but core and data migration should move only after detailed regulatory and linguistic validation. A common English-first group platform with translated labels will be inadequate.

---

# 6. Build a common product factory with local product ownership

The group should create reusable product components, but each local bank should approve the finished product.

## Common product chassis

Potential shared components include:

- account-opening workflow;
- savings goals;
- card controls;
- beneficiary management;
- transaction alerts;
- personal-finance views;
- SME invoicing and collections;
- merchant onboarding;
- loan application workflow;
- trade-service tracking;
- group-wide cyber and fraud controls.

## Mandatory local configuration

Each country should control:

- eligibility;
- KYC and identity evidence;
- interest and fee structure;
- credit policy;
- affordability tests;
- taxes and levies;
- transaction limits;
- payment rails;
- local currency;
- disclosure wording;
- language;
- complaint and redress process;
- collections;
- regulatory reporting;
- customer-data permissions.

## Cross-border propositions

The acquisition creates legitimate opportunities in:

- intra-African corporate banking;
- trade finance;
- cross-border payments;
- remittances;
- multicountry cash management;
- diaspora services;
- regional merchant settlement;
- corporate relationship coordination.

These should not be marketed as seamless until foreign-exchange rules, sanctions screening, capital controls, pricing transparency, settlement arrangements, and customer-redress responsibilities are clear.

A multinational customer may receive one relationship view while still contracting with separate local legal entities.

---

# 7. Use a staged brand strategy

Rebranding should follow service stability, not precede it.

## Stage 1: Ownership announcement

Communicate:

- ownership change;
- continuity of deposits and account access;
- continued local regulatory supervision;
- unchanged customer obligations unless explicitly notified;
- customer-service contacts;
- fraud warnings concerning false migration messages.

Avoid promising immediate product integration that has not been built or approved.

## Stage 2: Endorsed brand

A common transitional structure could be:

> “[Local Bank], a member of [Group]”

This preserves local recognition while introducing group scale.

Use the endorsed period to test:

- customer trust;
- awareness;
- employee adoption;
- corporate-client reactions;
- digital-channel performance;
- regulator concerns;
- confusion or fraud risk.

## Stage 3: Selective master-brand migration

Move to the group brand only if:

- the group brand is stronger or strategically valuable;
- the acquired name does not hold indispensable trust;
- legal and regulatory approvals are complete;
- channels and service standards are stable;
- customer research indicates limited attrition risk;
- trademark and linguistic checks are complete;
- customer support can absorb the change.

Countries need not rebrand on the same date. One acquired brand may remain as a protected local franchise while another migrates fully.

## Brand-decision scorecard

Assess:

- awareness and trust;
- customer acquisition cost;
- deposit-retention risk;
- corporate and government relationships;
- historical reputation;
- group-brand strength;
- language and pronunciation;
- trademark availability;
- re-signage and documentation cost;
- fraud and impersonation risk;
- strategic value of a pan-African identity.

Do not combine a legal-name change, app replacement, card reissuance, branch rationalization, and core migration in one customer event.

---

# 8. Align management without destroying local capability

Immediate displacement of local management can weaken regulator confidence, remove institutional knowledge, and encourage key employees and customers to leave. Equally, leaving all legacy structures untouched can prevent integration.

## Leadership model

For the first 12–18 months:

- retain credible local executives in critical roles;
- appoint a country integration director alongside, not above, the local CEO;
- place selected group leaders in control, architecture, and transformation roles;
- use joint local-group teams for product and technology delivery;
- document clear reserved decisions;
- perform fit-and-proper and succession reviews early;
- prepare succession rather than making abrupt removals except where control failures require action.

## Local CEO accountability

Country CEOs should be accountable for:

- the local regulator relationship;
- financial performance;
- capital and liquidity;
- customer outcomes;
- local talent;
- implementation of group controls;
- delivery of approved integration milestones.

They should not be rewarded only for resisting group change or, conversely, only for delivering short-term cost reductions.

## Group executive accountability

Group executives should be accountable for:

- reusable platforms;
- group risk standards;
- integration investment;
- cross-country synergies;
- architecture discipline;
- talent mobility;
- avoiding inappropriate centralization;
- timely resolution of local dependencies.

## Incentives

Use balanced integration scorecards covering:

- regulatory milestones;
- service availability;
- control remediation;
- customer retention;
- employee retention;
- digital adoption;
- synergy realization;
- migration quality;
- local profitability;
- conduct and complaints.

No executive should receive a migration bonus solely because a cutover occurred by the target date.

## Cultural integration

Conduct structured diagnostics covering:

- decision speed;
- hierarchy;
- escalation behavior;
- sales culture;
- risk ownership;
- regulator interaction;
- treatment of errors;
- branch versus digital power;
- central versus local authority.

Then establish shared management routines:

- monthly country-group operating reviews;
- joint risk committees;
- cross-country product squads;
- leadership exchanges;
- common control training;
- multilingual communications;
- protected channels for raising integration risks.

A single corporate-values workshop will not resolve entrenched management differences.

---

# 9. Consolidate markets and operations selectively

## Product rationalization

Classify existing products as:

- retain and scale;
- retain locally;
- migrate to a group equivalent;
- close to new business;
- retire after customer migration;
- remediate because terms or controls are deficient.

Before closing a product, assess:

- customer dependence;
- contractual notice;
- pricing changes;
- accrued interest;
- standing orders;
- linked collateral or credit;
- vulnerable customers;
- dormant balances;
- regulator expectations;
- complaints and remediation.

## Branch and service network

Do not use a group-wide branch-reduction percentage.

Evaluate each location by:

- profitability;
- deposits and lending relationships;
- cash demand;
- digital adoption;
- service alternatives;
- SME and corporate importance;
- regional inclusion;
- security;
- lease obligations;
- regulatory or political sensitivity.

Branch consolidation should follow improvements in digital reliability and customer-support capacity. It should not be used to fund digital integration before replacement channels work.

## Shared services

Early candidates for consolidation include:

- software procurement;
- selected security operations;
- vulnerability management;
- non-customer-sensitive analytics;
- architecture and development tooling;
- training platforms;
- selected finance processes;
- vendor management.

Later candidates, subject to regulatory approval and resilience testing, could include:

- payment operations;
- customer servicing;
- loan administration;
- financial-crime operations;
- data processing;
- disaster-recovery facilities.

Every intragroup service should have:

- a written service agreement;
- measurable service levels;
- audit and access rights;
- data and confidentiality provisions;
- business-continuity arrangements;
- exit and substitution plans;
- arm’s-length pricing;
- clear local management accountability.

The local bank cannot outsource its regulatory responsibility to the parent.

---

# 10. Protect customers during migration

## Customer-change principles

- Minimize mandatory action by customers.
- Preserve access to funds.
- Avoid silent changes to fees or terms.
- Give notices in the appropriate languages and channels.
- Provide clear effective dates.
- Separate genuine notices from phishing risks.
- Maintain accessible complaint and escalation paths.
- Offer enhanced support around cutovers.
- Identify vulnerable and digitally excluded customers.
- Monitor failed payments, declined cards, duplicate entries, and missing balances in real time.

## Migration communications

Every major change should explain:

- what is changing;
- what is not changing;
- whether credentials, cards, account numbers, or beneficiaries are affected;
- what the customer must do;
- when channels may be unavailable;
- how data will be used;
- where to seek help;
- how complaints will be resolved.

For high-risk changes, provide communications through several channels rather than relying only on in-app messages.

## Service recovery

Before cutover, establish:

- pre-approved fee refunds;
- emergency cash or payment procedures;
- priority handling for salary, pension, government, and business payments;
- corporate-client escalation teams;
- complaint evidence capture;
- automated identification of affected customers;
- root-cause and compensation governance.

---

# 11. Create regulator-specific integration plans

The group should not present one generic integration deck to all three supervisors.

For each jurisdiction, maintain a supervisory plan containing:

- legal-entity strategy;
- governance changes;
- capital and liquidity effects;
- proposed intragroup services;
- material outsourcing;
- system architecture;
- data-flow maps;
- cybersecurity controls;
- migration dates and gates;
- operational-resilience tests;
- customer communication;
- recovery and rollback plans;
- audit and assurance findings;
- post-implementation monitoring.

## Engagement cadence

Use:

1. early informal engagement;
2. formal submission where required;
3. technical workshops;
4. evidence-based readiness updates;
5. pre-cutover review;
6. immediate incident notification arrangements;
7. post-cutover reporting.

Regulators should not first learn of a material group-platform dependency shortly before launch.

The group regulatory office should coordinate consistency, but local regulatory teams should lead the relationship and ensure that representations are accurate for the local entity.

---

# 12. Establish explicit migration gates

A major release or core migration should require approval across six gates.

## Gate 1: Regulatory

- required approvals or non-objections obtained;
- commitments documented;
- outsourcing and data questions resolved.

## Gate 2: Technology

- capacity and performance proven;
- cybersecurity tests complete;
- disaster recovery demonstrated;
- monitoring and support operating.

## Gate 3: Data and finance

- balances reconciled;
- interest and fee calculations validated;
- general-ledger mapping complete;
- regulatory reports run in parallel;
- data exceptions below threshold.

## Gate 4: Operations

- procedures documented;
- staff trained;
- vendors ready;
- contact centers and branches prepared;
- incident command structure rehearsed.

## Gate 5: Customer and conduct

- notices approved and delivered;
- vulnerable-customer impacts assessed;
- complaint and compensation processes ready;
- pricing and disclosures validated.

## Gate 6: Independent assurance

- internal audit, risk assurance, or an independent reviewer has challenged readiness;
- unresolved critical findings have been closed or formally accepted by the appropriate local authority.

Commercial pressure should not override a failed gate.

---

# 13. Sequence synergies by risk

## Early, lower-risk synergies

- software and infrastructure procurement;
- common cybersecurity tooling;
- vendor consolidation;
- shared development standards;
- group insurance and selected professional services;
- common training;
- management reporting definitions;
- treasury coordination within local restrictions;
- reusable digital components.

## Medium-term synergies

- product-platform reuse;
- shared fraud intelligence;
- regional corporate-client coverage;
- selected operations centers;
- common CRM and workflow;
- cross-border payments and trade services;
- branch and real-estate optimization.

## Later, higher-risk synergies

- core replacement;
- centralization of transaction processing;
- cross-border customer-data platforms;
- legal-entity restructuring;
- large-scale workforce reductions;
- full brand replacement;
- consolidation of disaster-recovery infrastructure.

The synergy business case should include:

- migration and dual-running costs;
- regulator-required remediation;
- customer compensation;
- employee retention;
- data cleansing;
- decommissioning;
- contract termination;
- tax;
- local service requirements;
- ongoing control costs.

Synergies should be reported only when realized, not when a legacy platform is scheduled for retirement.

